<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; border: 2px solid #1a5276;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<br>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Importance of Water</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How We Use Water Everyday</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Comprehend multi-step instructions for academic tasks. Interactive: Respond to questions and requests in a school setting.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow multi-step instructions about water usage in a school setting (e.g., "Please turn off the tap.")
            Can respond to questions about water conservation (e.g., "Why is it important to save water?" "If we don't save water, we will face water shortages.")
            Can identify details from a spoken explanation of the importance of water (e.g., "What do we use water for?" "For cooking, drinking, and agriculture.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activating prior knowledge and introducing the topic of daily water usage.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows students a picture of a glass of potable water and asks, "What is this?" (Water). Then shows flashcards or displays images of people doing different daily activities: drinking, cooking, washing hands, showering, watering plants. The teacher asks, "What are they doing with water?" Students listen and respond with simple actions or point to pictures. The teacher introduces key verbs: "drink," "cook," "wash," "use," and the phrase "We use water for...".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of core vocabulary words: "water," "drink," "cook," "wash," "save," "tap," "important." The teacher models sentences expressing purpose: "We use water for drinking." "We use water for cooking." "We use water for cleaning." The teacher also models the stress pattern for compound words like "forecast" (stress on the first syllable) and "important" (stress on the second syllable), asking students to listen carefully to the stressed part.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using concept checking questions focusing on listening comprehension and meaning.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we use water for sleeping?" (Expected answer: No.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is water important for life?" (Expected answer: Yes.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do we use water for?" (Expected answer: Drinking, cooking, cleaning, etc.)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to "The Importance of Water."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher plays a short (1-2 minute) audio clip (e.g., a simple public service announcement or a short informative recording) about "The Importance of Water" and "How We Use Water Everyday." The audio clip should introduce vocabulary like "resource," "limited," "save," "conserve," "shortage," and include grammar structures like "We use water for drinking, cooking, and cleaning" and "We save water because it is a limited resource." (A2.2 level: clear, slow speech).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Comprehend general and specific information from short, simple spoken texts on familiar topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Listening focus):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> The teacher asks, "What is the audio mainly about?" (Expected: Water and why it's important). "What should we do with water?" (Expected: Save/conserve it).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students listen to the audio again. The teacher reads simple statements based on the audio, and students say "True" or "False."
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We use water for playing games." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Water is an important resource." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We should waste water." (False)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> The teacher displays pictures of activities (e.g., a person drinking, a person showering, someone watering plants). Students listen to the audio and raise the correct picture when they hear the activity mentioned.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening, integrating new vocabulary and grammar.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> First conditional ("If we don't save water, we will face water shortages."), For + verb + ing to express purpose ("We use water for drinking, cooking, and cleaning."), Subject + verb + complement + because + cause ("We save water because it is a limited resource.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> water, resource, shortage, tap, save, drink, waste, use, essential, limited, important, daily, carefully.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Practice stress patterns for "forecast," "important"; basic syllabification for "resource," "limited."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Expressing purpose of an action.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure (Listening-focused activities):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listen and Complete (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with sentences containing gaps. The teacher reads out each sentence clearly, twice. Students listen carefully and write down the missing target vocabulary word.
                        <br><i>Examples:</i>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We use _______ for drinking." (water)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Water is an important _______." (resource)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If we don't save water, we will have a _______." (shortage)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We save water _______ it is a limited resource." (because)</li>
                        </ul>
                        Students then listen as the teacher repeats the correct answers, checking their work.
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listen and Identify (Semi-controlled Practice):</b> The teacher says a sentence using target grammar or vocabulary. Then, the teacher says two options, and students listen and identify which option is correct or matches the initial sentence.
                        <br><i>Examples:</i>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says: "We use water for cooking." Then: "A) Water for cooking. B) Water for dancing." Students identify A.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says: "If we waste water, there will be a shortage." Then: "A) If we waste water... B) If we save water..." Students identify A.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says: "Water is an essential resource." Students listen for stress patterns and count syllables (e.g., 'es-SEN-tial').</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Listening and Checking:</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides a list of sentence starters related to water use and conservation (e.g., "We use water for...", "If we don't save water...", "Water is important because..."). Partner A says a complete sentence using a starter and including target vocabulary and grammar. Partner B listens for correctness and clarity, especially the pronunciation of key words and stress patterns. Then they switch roles. This allows for peer-to-peer listening practice.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students use the language in a realistic scenario related to water conservation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small Group Discussion and Reporting.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups (3-4 students).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays a short (30-second) audio scenario: "Imagine our school has a water shortage. What can we do to save water daily?" The audio includes a prompt: "If we don't save water, what will happen?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group discusses solutions for water conservation in a school setting, focusing on using the first conditional ("If we turn off the tap, we will save water.") and expressing purpose ("We could save water by turning off taps while washing hands."). They also practice expressing cause and effect ("We need to save water because it is limited.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After 5-7 minutes of discussion, each group chooses one representative to report their main ideas to the class. The rest of the class listens to identify different solutions.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in discussing solutions, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion. Students actively listen to the scenario and their group members, and then to other groups' reports.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy to check listening comprehension and initial production of target language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment during group discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Following Instructions:</b> The teacher gives a multi-step instruction related to water use: "First, listen to these two sentences. Second, tell your partner which sentence explains why water is important. Third, write down one way to save water at school." Students' ability to follow these spoken instructions will be observed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Responding to Questions:</b> The teacher asks individuals questions like, "Why is it important to save water?" or "What do we use water for daily?" Students respond orally, demonstrating their listening comprehension and ability to formulate an answer using target grammar and vocabulary.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to follow multi-step spoken instructions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of key vocabulary (e.g., "important," "resource," "shortage").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., "save," "waste," "tap," "limited," "essential").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of responses when explaining purpose ("for + verb+ing") or cause ("because").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and active listening during group discussions.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket by listening to the teacher's prompts and then silently reflecting or writing short answers.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I understand simple instructions about saving water?" (Yes/No/A little)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I say why water is important?" (Yes/No/A little)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reflecting on the learning process and planning for future lessons.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words about water did I learn today by listening?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I use water more carefully at home and at school, based on what we discussed?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the listening activities worked well? (e.g., audio clarity, engagement with flashcards, specific practice drills).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed for students who struggled with multi-step instructions or identifying details from the audio? (e.g., slower speech, more repetition, visual cues).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the target vocabulary and grammar structures understood in context? If not, how can I reinforce them?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson introduced core vocabulary and concepts through listening. The next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension) will build on this foundation by presenting the same vocabulary and grammar in a written text, allowing students to deepen their understanding of "The Importance of Water" through reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>